# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
Corpus for the admissions-essay AI detector.

IMPORTANT / HONESTY NOTE (see also the project report):
This sandbox has no internet access, so this corpus could not be scraped
or pulled from an existing dataset. Every essay below was written for this
project to *stand in* for two populations:

  HUMAN_TRAIN / HUMAN_TEST  - short personal-statement-style essays written
                               to imitate genuine, imperfect student prose:
                               uneven sentence length, concrete specific
                               detail, occasional fragments, informal voice.

  AI_TRAIN / AI_TEST        - essays deliberately written in the *formulaic*
                               register that current LLMs fall into on this
                               task when asked for a polished admissions
                               essay: hedged claims, triadic lists, stock
                               transitions ("Moreover", "It is important to
                               note"), uniform sentence length, generic
                               uplifting closers, elevated near-synonyms
                               ("delve", "underscore", "testament",
                               "tapestry", "boundless").

  ESL_TEST                  - short essays written to imitate a genuinely
                               common human population: fluent but
                               non-native English writers, who often produce
                               simpler, more repetitive sentence structure.
                               This set exists specifically to check whether
                               the detector mistakes "simple and repetitive"
                               for "machine-written" (a documented failure
                               mode of real AI detectors).

  MIXED_DEMO                - one essay with a human first half and an
                               AI-polished second half, used as the default
                               text in the app so the sentence-level
                               highlighting has something real to show.

This is a small, synthetic, single-author (well, single-model) corpus. It is
not a substitute for a real dataset scraped from many writers and many
models, and the report is explicit about what that means for how much to
trust the numbers below.
"""

HUMAN_TRAIN = [
"""My grandmother taught me to cook the summer I turned twelve, mostly because she got tired of me hovering in the kitchen doorway asking what everything was. We made pierogi from scratch, and I remember the dough sticking to everything, my hands, the counter, even the dog's nose when he wandered too close. She didn't measure anything. When I asked how much flour, she just said "enough," and somehow that was an answer. I burned the first batch of onions so badly the smoke alarm went off twice. She laughed, opened a window, and started over without a word of complaint. That's the thing about her kitchen: mistakes were just part of the recipe, never a reason to stop.""",

"""I lost the regional debate final by one point, on a technicality about rebuttal timing that I still think was called wrong. For about a week I was furious about it, replaying the round in my head on the bus, in the shower, during math class. Then my coach asked me a question that annoyed me at the time: "What would you do differently if the score didn't matter?" I didn't have a good answer, which bothered me more than the loss did. So I went back and rewatched the recording, and I noticed I'd rushed my closing argument because I was scared of running out of time. That's a fixable problem. The score wasn't.""",

"""Our town's only movie theater closed the spring of my sophomore year, and honestly I didn't think much of it at first. Then I noticed my after-school routine had a hole in it. A few of us started a "movie night" in Dana's garage instead, projector taped to a bedsheet, folding chairs from three different houses. It was worse in almost every technical way. The sound cut out during the scary parts. But somehow more people showed up each week, kids I barely knew from other grades, because it turned out what we missed wasn't really the theater, it was having somewhere to be on a Friday.""",

"""I work weekends at my uncle's auto shop, mostly doing oil changes and tire rotations, occasionally handed something harder when he's short-staffed. Last winter a woman brought in her car making a noise she described as "like a dying bird, but angrier." I had no idea what that meant mechanically, and neither did my uncle at first. Turned out to be a loose heat shield rattling against the exhaust, a two-minute fix once we found it, forty minutes to find it. I liked that more than I expected to, the part where the answer isn't obvious and you have to actually listen for it.""",

"""My little brother has a stutter, and for years I was the sibling who finished his sentences for him, thinking I was helping. A speech therapist eventually pulled me aside and told me, pretty bluntly, that I wasn't. She said every time I jumped in, I was telling him his own voice wasn't worth waiting for. I felt awful, honestly, and a little defensive. But I started counting to five in my head before saying anything when he talked. Some days that's still hard. He talks more now, though, and slower, and I've learned that silence isn't the same thing as a problem.""",

"""Three summers of detasseling corn taught me more about heat than any thermometer could. You start at five in the morning to beat it, walking the rows before the sun is fully up, and by ten your shirt is soaked through and you still have six hours left. I complained constantly the first summer. By the third, I'd figured out which rows had shade at which hour, how to pace myself so I didn't crash by noon, small things nobody teaches you, you just learn them by getting it wrong enough times. My dad still does this work every summer. I understand him a little better now than I used to.""",

"""I didn't get into the honors chemistry track sophomore year, which stung more than I expected given that I'm not even sure I like chemistry. I emailed the teacher asking what I could do differently, half expecting a form response. Instead she wrote back three paragraphs, and one line stuck with me: "You're strong on the math, weak on writing up your reasoning, and that's the actual skill this class tests." I hadn't thought of lab reports as a writing skill before. I started treating them that way, badly at first, and by spring I'd talked my way into the class after all, on the strength of one very over-explained report on reaction rates.""",

"""My family moved four times before I turned ten, which people usually assume made me an easy talker, good at meeting people fast. It did the opposite, actually. I got tired of investing in friendships that had an expiration date I couldn't control, so by the third move I mostly kept to myself. It took a teacher noticing I ate lunch alone every day for a semester before I really opened up again, and even then it was slow, a few words at a time. I'm better at it now, but I still remember exactly which of my current friends I decided, consciously, to let myself get attached to.""",

"""The community garden plot behind the library was mostly weeds when four of us took it over freshman year. None of us knew anything about growing vegetables. We killed the first round of tomatoes by overwatering them, which felt impossible given how dry the soil looked on top. An older woman who tended the plot next to ours eventually took pity on us and explained soil drainage in a way that finally made sense, with her hands more than her words, really, pushing a finger into the dirt to show us what "too wet" actually felt like. We got real tomatoes the second year. I still don't consider myself good at gardening, but I know what wet dirt is supposed to feel like now, which is apparently most of the battle.""",

"""I've kept the same paper journal since eighth grade, not because I'm disciplined about it, I'm really not, some months go by with nothing, but because I like having a record of what I actually thought at the time instead of what I remember thinking. Reading back through it is occasionally mortifying. Freshman year me had extremely strong opinions about a boy band that fourteen-year-old me apparently thought were going to matter. But it's also useful. I can see, in writing, exactly when I stopped being scared of public speaking, which was one specific Tuesday in tenth grade, not a gradual thing at all like I would have guessed.""",

"""My part-time job is answering phones at a veterinary clinic, which sounds calm and is almost never calm. People call in a genuine panic about their dogs eating socks, chocolate, once memorably an entire bar of soap. I used to just transfer everything straight to the vet tech, overwhelmed by how upset people got over what was often, medically, not that serious. Six months in I can usually tell from the first thirty seconds whether it's an emergency or just a scared owner who needs someone calm on the other end of the line. Mostly it's the second one. Being that calm voice turned out to be its own kind of useful.""",

"""I ran track for two years before I actually got fast, and honestly the two facts might not be related. I was fast the same season I stopped caring so much about being fast, which is annoying advice to give anyone but happens to be true in my case. Before that I'd tighten up before every race, overthink my start, false-start twice in one meet out of pure nerves. My coach eventually told me to stop watching the clock during practice entirely for a month. It felt like giving something up. My times dropped anyway, which I still don't fully understand, but I've stopped arguing with it.""",

"""My grandfather doesn't talk much about the war, and for a long time I didn't ask, assuming it was a closed subject. Then last year, helping him clean out the garage, I found a box of letters he'd written to my grandmother during that time, and he let me read a few. They were nothing like I expected, mostly about food he missed and a dog he'd had before, small ordinary things, not the big dramatic moments I'd imagined. I asked him about it afterward and he just said, "that's what you think about, actually, when the big stuff is too much." I think about that a lot now.""",

"""Sophomore year I signed up for the school's peer tutoring program mostly because I needed volunteer hours, not out of any noble instinct. My first student was a sixth grader who hated math with a real, specific intensity, and for the first few sessions I genuinely could not get through to him. I tried three different explanations of fractions before one, involving pizza slices and his little sister's greed about them, finally landed. He still doesn't love math. But he stopped crying during our sessions, which felt like an actual accomplishment at the time, and honestly still does.""",

"""I've worked the register at my family's restaurant since I was old enough to reach it, which means I've watched a lot of first dates go badly. There's a specific silence that means it's not working out, different from the comfortable kind. I started, without really meaning to, timing my visits to the table around those silences, coming by with water refills right when a conversation needed rescuing. My dad noticed and started calling it "customer service," which felt generous. I just didn't like watching people be awkward in my family's restaurant. Turns out that's most of what customer service is anyway.""",

"""My school cut the art program the same year I'd finally saved up for a decent set of paints, which felt like extremely bad timing, though I know that's not really how budget decisions work. A few of us petitioned to keep at least the after-school club running, unpaid, in a spare classroom, and the vice principal agreed mostly, I think, to make us stop emailing him. It's a smaller thing than a real program. We meet twice a week, no curriculum, whoever wants to teach something that week just does. I've learned more there, somehow, than I did in the class it replaced, though I'd still take the funding back if I could.""",

"""I spent a summer teaching my little cousin to swim, which I agreed to before realizing I'm not actually a strong swimmer myself, just stronger than a six-year-old. She was terrified of putting her face in the water, would grip the pool ladder like it owed her money. What finally worked wasn't any technique I'd read about, it was letting her hold onto me instead of the ladder for the first two weeks, just so she'd trust that going under wasn't the same as drowning. By August she was doing laps, badly, arms everywhere, but laps. I don't think I taught her to swim so much as I taught her to stop being scared long enough to figure it out herself.""",

"""My debate partner and I disagree about almost everything outside of debate, politics, music, where to eat before tournaments, and I think that's exactly why we won as often as we did. She'd build an argument I hadn't considered because she genuinely believed a version of the other side, not as a exercise, actually believed it, and I'd have to actually engage with it instead of just performing disagreement. Most of our best rounds came out of arguments we'd already half-had with each other in the car on the way to the tournament. I don't know that I'd have gotten as good working with someone who agreed with me more.""",

"""I broke my arm the week before regional swim finals, which is about the least convenient possible timing an injury can have, and spent the next six weeks on deck instead of in the water, timing splits for the teammates who could actually swim. It was miserable at first, standing there with a stopwatch while everyone else did the thing I'd trained for all year. But watching that closely, from outside the water instead of in it, I noticed technical mistakes in my own stroke I'd never caught on video, small stuff about my hip rotation. I came back slower than before the injury and somehow swam a faster time than I ever had, three months later, for reasons I'm still not sure I fully understand.""",

"""My neighbor is ninety-one and still mows his own lawn, refuses help on principle as far as I can tell. Last spring I started "accidentally" leaving my mower out where he'd see it, hoping he'd take the hint that borrowing it was easier than hauling his own out of the shed. He never took the bait. Eventually I just started showing up on Saturdays to help, framing it as wanting company, which wasn't entirely a lie. He tells the same three stories every time, almost word for word, and I've started to look forward to which one it'll be that week.""",
]

